display: "Match content frame rate: Never" stops apps switching the display mode, and is the default

On a DP/HDMI monitor every display mode change is a full link re-sync: the screen goes black for
1-3 s. Games often ask for another refresh rate when they change scene (for example 30 fps for a
menu), and with a 4K60 monitor that offers 4K30/25/24 too, Android switched the monitor to 4K30
and back each time.

Upstream, Settings.Secure.match_content_frame_rate = 0 ("Never", SWITCHING_TYPE_NONE) only pins
the refresh-rate ranges to the base mode after the base mode has been chosen, and the base mode
is still chosen from the app's preferredDisplayModeId / preferredRefreshRate vote. The user's
min/peak refresh-rate settings rank below that vote, so nothing a user can set stops it.

With SWITCHING_TYPE_NONE the app-request votes (render range, base mode, size) are now ignored,
and NONE is the default when the setting has never been written, so the display stays in its
default mode (the monitor's preferred one, e.g. 3840x2160@60). Apps still render at the frame
rate they want; only the monitor mode stays fixed.

To let apps switch modes again on a running board:
  adb shell settings put secure match_content_frame_rate 1   (seamless only)
  adb shell settings put secure match_content_frame_rate 2   (always, upstream behaviour)
Build without this patch: build.ps1 -AllowModeSwitch / build.sh --allow-mode-switch.

